export const ENRICH_FESTIVALS: EnrichmentSet = {
  'maha-shivratri': {
    about: [
      `Shivratri means the night of Shiva, and the tradition counts twelve of them in a year, one on the dark fourteenth of each lunar month. The one called Maha, the great, is tied to Phalguna Krishna Paksha in the data for this page. Shaiva texts such as the Shiva Purana and the Linga Purana describe it as a night for staying awake, and the idea of vigil is the heart of it: the mind is asked to remain turned toward Shiva while the world sleeps.`,
      `Several stories are attached to the night. One tells of Shiva appearing as an endless pillar of fire, the linga, whose top and base neither Brahma nor Vishnu could find. Another, popular in many households, is the tale of a hunter who spent the night in a bilva tree, dropping leaves onto a linga below without any intention of worship, and was still credited with merit. Popular belief also links the night to the marriage of Shiva and Parvati, which is how the date is understood in many families.`,
      `Practice differs by region. In Kashmir the festival, called Herath, is a major household event for Pandit families, while in South India temples such as those of the Shaiva centres hold all-night programmes with music and dance. The night is traditionally divided into four prahars, each with its own worship. Those who keep a fast should follow what is comfortable for their health, since the tradition itself values sincerity over severity.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why are bilva leaves offered to Shiva?`, a: `In Shaiva tradition the trifoliate bilva leaf is regarded as dear to Shiva, and some commentators read its three leaflets as the three eyes or the three gunas. It is a long-standing custom rather than a rule that applies in every region.` },
      { q: `What is the difference between Shivratri and Maha Shivratri?`, a: `Shivratri recurs monthly on the dark fourteenth tithi, while Maha Shivratri is the annual one, placed in the month of Phalguna in the common reckoning of north India.` },
    ],
  },
  'holi': {
    about: [
      `Holi is really two observances joined together. The first evening is Holika Dahan, when a bonfire is lit, and the next day is the playing of colours, often called Rangwali Holi or Dhulandi. The bonfire recalls the Puranic story of Prahlada, the child devotee of Vishnu, whose aunt Holika sat in a fire with him on her lap, expecting him to burn. According to the story it was she who perished while he was untouched, and the fire is therefore read as the burning of arrogance and cruelty.`,
      `The colour half of the festival carries a different set of memories, centred on the play of Krishna and Radha in Braj. Villages around Mathura and Vrindavan stretch the celebration over many days, and Barsana and Nandgaon are known for the playful Lathmar Holi, in which women chase men with sticks while men shield themselves. Elsewhere in India the emphasis shifts: in Bengal it appears as Dol Jatra, with swings for the deity, and in Maharashtra and parts of the south people mark the fire more than the colour.`,
      `Because the festival arrives with the end of winter, it also works as a seasonal threshold, and old customs involve clearing the household of old things, settling quarrels and visiting neighbours. Natural colours made from flowers or turmeric are a gentler choice than industrial dyes, and protecting eyes and skin is sensible common courtesy at any gathering.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is a bonfire lit before Holi?`, a: `The bonfire of Holika Dahan re-enacts the story of Holika and Prahlada from the Puranas, in which devotion survives the fire that was meant to destroy it.` },
      { q: `Is Holi celebrated on the same day everywhere?`, a: `Not always. The fire is lit on the eve and colours are played the following day in much of north India, but local calendars and customs such as Dol Jatra in Bengal can shift the timing.` },
    ],
  },
  'ram-navami': {
    about: [
      `Ram Navami remembers the birth of Rama to King Dasharatha and Queen Kaushalya in Ayodhya. The story is told in the Valmiki Ramayana, where Dasharatha performs a putrakameshti sacrifice and receives a sacred offering shared among his queens, after which Rama, Bharata, Lakshmana and Shatrughna are born. Tulsidas retells the birth in the Ramcharitmanas in the vernacular, and that text is what most north Indian households read and sing from during the festival.`,
      `The tradition places Rama's birth at midday on the ninth of Chaitra Shukla Paksha, so many temples hold their main ceremony around noon, when a cradle scene is arranged and the infant image is rocked. Ramayana recitations, sometimes completed over nine days, and the singing of bhajans are common. In Tamil Nadu and Andhra Pradesh the day is also observed as the wedding anniversary of Rama and Sita, and a ceremonial wedding is held in temples such as Bhadrachalam in Telangana. A special panakam drink of jaggery and pepper and a cooling kosambari are served in the south.`,
      `The festival closes the nine days of Chaitra Navaratri in many regions, so it sits at the meeting point of Rama devotion and the worship of the Goddess. Rama is presented in the tradition as Maryada Purushottam, the person who keeps right conduct even when it costs him, and that idea of ideal conduct is what homilies on the day tend to dwell on.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is Ram Navami celebrated at noon?`, a: `Tradition holds that Rama was born at midday, so the principal worship and cradle ceremony in many temples is timed close to noon.` },
      { q: `Which text is usually read on Ram Navami?`, a: `The Valmiki Ramayana and the Ramcharitmanas of Tulsidas are the most common choices, often read in part or as a nine-day recital.` },
    ],
  },
  'hanuman-jayanti': {
    about: [
      `Hanuman Jayanti marks the birth of Hanuman, the son of Anjana and Kesari and, in tradition, of Vayu the wind god in a spiritual sense. Because the story of his birth is retold in different ways across the Puranas and regional folk accounts, the date itself is not uniform in India. The data here gives the full moon of Chaitra, which is the north Indian reckoning, whereas in Tamil Nadu Hanuman Jayanti is observed in the month of Margazhi on the day of the Moola nakshatra, and in Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka other dates are followed.`,
      `The tales told on the day are the familiar ones: the child Hanuman mistaking the sun for a fruit and leaping toward it, the leap across the ocean to Lanka, the carrying of the Sanjeevani mountain, and above all his unwavering service to Rama. These stories make him the model of devotion joined with strength and humility, which is why wrestlers, athletes and students often hold him as a patron. Hanuman is also traditionally regarded as a chiranjivi, one of the immortals, which gives the festival the feeling of a celebration of a presence rather than a memory.`,
      `Temples often open before sunrise with abhishek and a heavy coating of sindoor and oil, and the Hanuman Chalisa of Tulsidas is recited, sometimes many times in a row. Tuesdays and Saturdays are the regular days of his worship, so the festival is a concentrated version of an ordinary weekly habit. Wherever sindoor is used, it is best to follow the custom of the local temple.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Is Hanuman Jayanti a birthday or a victory day?`, a: `Traditionally it is the birth celebration, although in some regions, such as parts of Odisha and Andhra, a different date is kept and the emphasis varies.` },
      { q: `Why is sindoor offered to Hanuman?`, a: `A well-known folk story says Hanuman applied sindoor over his body after seeing Sita wear it for Rama's long life. Offering it is a popular custom, not a requirement.` },
    ],
  },
  'guru-purnima': {
    about: [
      `Guru Purnima belongs to the full moon of Ashadha, a time when the monsoon begins and the wandering ascetics of old were expected to settle in one place for the rainy months. That settling is what gave students a season for study with a teacher, and the full moon became the day on which they honoured him. The Hindu tradition links the day to Veda Vyasa, who is said to have arranged the Vedas and composed the Mahabharata and the Puranas, which is why it is also known as Vyasa Purnima.`,
      `The same full moon is observed among Buddhists as the day of the first sermon at Sarnath, and among Jains as the time Mahavira is remembered as accepting his first disciple, so the day carries meaning across several Indian traditions. In Hindu practice the emphasis falls on gratitude: a disciple visits the teacher, offers flowers, fruit or a gift, and receives instruction or a blessing. Lineages that revere a guru parampara, a chain of teachers going back centuries, often hold gatherings where the chain is recited by name.`,
      `The idea is wider than religion. Musicians and dancers in the classical arts perform a guru puja for their teachers, and students of Sanskrit and yoga pay respects in the same spirit. There is also a long Indian saying, found in the Guru Gita, that places the guru on a par with the divine, but it is best read as an expression of respect for a good teacher. Anyone without a formal guru can still remember a parent, a school teacher or an author who shaped them.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is Guru Purnima also called Vyasa Purnima?`, a: `Because tradition honours Veda Vyasa as the compiler of the Vedas and author of the Mahabharata, the first great teacher of the scriptural lineage.` },
      { q: `Do I need a guru to observe it?`, a: `No. Many people simply thank a teacher, a parent or a scripture they learned from, and recite a short verse of respect.` },
    ],
  },
  'krishna-janmashtami': {
    about: [
      `The birth of Krishna is told in the Bhagavata Purana and the Vishnu Purana. His parents Devaki and Vasudeva were imprisoned by her brother Kamsa, the tyrant of Mathura, after a prophecy said her eighth child would end his rule. Krishna is born at midnight in the prison, the chains loosen, and Vasudeva carries the infant across the swollen Yamuna to Gokul, where he is exchanged for the daughter of Yashoda and Nanda. This is the reason the main celebration takes place at midnight and not at dawn.`,
      `Observance varies widely. In Mathura, Vrindavan and Dwarka, temples are decorated heavily and the birth is celebrated with a midnight arati and the rocking of a cradle. In Maharashtra and Gujarat the data points to the Dahi Handi custom, in which teams form human pyramids to break a pot of curd hung high above the street, recalling Krishna's boyhood love of butter. In Manipur and Odisha, dance and drama traditions take their own forms, and in South India households draw tiny footprints leading into the home to welcome the child.`,
      `Because the day is also the occasion to remember the person who spoke the Bhagavad Gita, many communities read from it in the afternoon. Some Vaishnava sects follow a different tithi or lunar convention from Smartas, so two neighbouring temples may sometimes keep the festival on consecutive days. Families who fast often break the vrata only after the midnight birth, but a lighter fast is perfectly acceptable for children, elders and people with health concerns.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is Janmashtami celebrated at midnight?`, a: `The Puranas place Krishna's birth at midnight in Kamsa's prison, so devotees stay awake until that hour before opening the fast.` },
      { q: `What is Dahi Handi?`, a: `It is a custom in which groups build a human pyramid to reach and break a hanging pot of curd, echoing the stories of the butter-loving child Krishna.` },
    ],
  },
  'ganesh-chaturthi': {
    about: [
      `Ganesha is known as Vighnaharta, the remover of obstacles, and the festival to him is among the most visible in India. The Puranas offer several accounts of his origin. In the best known, Parvati forms a child from the paste of her own body to guard her bath, and after a confrontation with Shiva the boy is restored with the head of an elephant. The birthday celebration is placed on Bhadrapada Shukla Chaturthi in the data, the fourth day of the waxing moon.`,
      `Household worship of Ganesha is old, but the large public form of the festival is relatively recent. The data notes that Lokmanya Tilak popularised public celebration in the 1890s, turning a family worship into a community gathering with processions, community pandals and cultural programmes in the freedom movement. Today Maharashtra, Goa and Karnataka lead in scale, and Mumbai and Pune are known for their enormous images. In Tamil Nadu the day is called Vinayaka Chaturthi and is often more homely, with kozhukattai sweets steamed for the deity.`,
      `Clay images are traditionally modelled by hand, installed, worshipped for a chosen period of one and a half, three, five, seven or ten days, and then immersed in water, a gesture meant to recall the return of all forms to the formless. In recent years many cities have encouraged natural clay images and artificial immersion tanks to reduce river pollution. A small clay Ganesha at home is a simple and ecological choice.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is Ganesha immersed in water at the end?`, a: `The immersion, or visarjan, is understood as sending the deity back to his abode and as a reminder that every form eventually dissolves.` },
      { q: `Do all families keep the idol for ten days?`, a: `No. Many households keep it for one and a half, three, five or seven days, while the ten-day period is common for public pandals.` },
    ],
  },
  'navaratri': {
    about: [
      `Navaratri means nine nights, and the autumn one begins on Ashwin Shukla Pratipada according to this page. It is the great season of the Goddess, and the principal text recited is the Devi Mahatmya, also called the Durga Saptashati, part of the Markandeya Purana. In it the Goddess takes form from the combined energies of the gods and defeats the buffalo demon Mahishasura, as well as Shumbha and Nishumbha, who could not be killed by any ordinary power.`,
      `The nine days are given to the Nava Durga forms, from Shailaputri to Siddhidatri, though other schemes exist. Gujarat is famous for the garba and dandiya dances performed around a lamp or an image of the Goddess in the evenings. In Bengal the same period is the lead-up to Durga Puja, in Mysuru and Kullu it merges with the royal Dussehra processions, and in Tamil Nadu and Karnataka homes arrange golu or bombe habba, displays of dolls on tiered steps. In many South Indian families the last three days honour Lakshmi and Saraswati, and books and tools are worshipped on Ayudha Puja.`,
      `Seen as a season, Navaratri falls at the change from rain to the clear autumn, and Ayurvedic and folk reasoning treat the fasting and plainer food as a way to adjust the body between seasons. Fasting rules differ by household: some eat once a day, some live on fruit and milk, and some keep a full fast only on certain days. Choose what is manageable and keep it gentle.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What are the nine forms of Durga?`, a: `The usual list is Shailaputri, Brahmacharini, Chandraghanta, Kushmanda, Skandamata, Katyayani, Kalaratri, Mahagauri and Siddhidatri, one for each night.` },
      { q: `Is Navaratri celebrated twice a year?`, a: `Yes. Besides the autumn Sharada Navaratri there is Chaitra Navaratri in spring, and some traditions also count two Gupta Navaratris.` },
    ],
  },
  'diwali': {
    about: [
      `Diwali, from Deepavali, the row of lamps, is a five-day season rather than a single evening, and its stories vary by region. In much of north India it recalls the return of Rama to Ayodhya after fourteen years of exile, when citizens lit lamps to welcome him. In many homes across India the dominant theme is Lakshmi, who according to the Puranas rose from the churning of the ocean of milk, and her worship takes place on the new-moon evening, which is the main night of the festival.`,
      `Other threads are woven in. In South India the day before is commonly marked as Naraka Chaturdashi, which remembers Krishna's defeat of the demon Naraka, and people bathe in oil before dawn. In Bengal the new moon night belongs to Kali, in Gujarat the new year of the Vikram calendar starts the next day, and traders in many regions open fresh account books in a ceremony called Chopda Pujan, which is the practice behind the line in the data about starting new ledgers. Jains remember Mahavira's liberation on a related night, and Sikhs observe Bandi Chhor Divas, which shows how widely the lights are shared.`,
      `The five days normally run from Dhanteras through Naraka Chaturdashi, Lakshmi Puja and Govardhan Puja to Bhai Dooj. Preparations include cleaning and whitewashing the house, drawing rangoli at the threshold, and exchanging sweets with neighbours. Where firecrackers are a concern for air quality or for pets and elders, many families now choose lamps and low-noise celebrations.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is Lakshmi worshipped on Diwali?`, a: `Many traditions link the festival to Lakshmi, who in the Puranas emerged during the churning of the ocean, and her worship is placed on the new-moon night.` },
      { q: `Which five days make up Diwali?`, a: `Commonly Dhanteras, Naraka Chaturdashi, Lakshmi Puja, Govardhan Puja and Bhai Dooj, though local calendars may shift or merge them.` },
    ],
  },
  'chhath-puja': {
    about: [
      `Chhath is among the few Hindu festivals directed purely at the Sun, with no idol, and it is also dedicated to Chhathi Maiya, a goddess associated with children and often identified with Shashthi or Usha. The name points to the sixth day of the bright half of Kartika, which is where the main rite falls. It is strongest in Bihar, Jharkhand, eastern Uttar Pradesh and Nepal's Terai, and communities from those regions have carried it to Mumbai, Delhi and abroad.`,
      `Tradition connects the worship to several figures. Some accounts say that Draupadi and the Pandavas kept a Surya vrata for relief from hardship, and others link it to Karna, a son of Surya, who is said to have stood in water offering arghya every dawn. Yet another account is in the Ramayana tradition, which says Sita observed the rite after the return from Lanka. Because versions differ, it is better to treat these as layered memories rather than a single canonical origin.`,
      `The four days run as follows: a day of bathing and a simple meal of rice and bottle gourd, a day of kharna with sweet kheer after fasting through the day, the evening arghya to the setting sun, and finally the dawn arghya to the rising sun. Offerings are carried in bamboo baskets and include thekua, a wheat sweet, fruit and sugarcane. The data describes a thirty-six hour waterless fast, which is physically demanding, so those who are not well should consider a modified observance. The riverbank scenes, with ghats cleaned and lit by lamps, are a social effort led by neighbourhoods.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is arghya in Chhath?`, a: `Arghya is an offering of water, milk and fruits made while standing in water, first at sunset and then at sunrise during the festival.` },
      { q: `Is there an idol of the Sun in Chhath?`, a: `No. The worship is directed at the natural sun and the river, without a temple image, along with Chhathi Maiya.` },
    ],
  },
  'makar-sankranti': {
    about: [
      `Unlike most Hindu festivals, Makar Sankranti follows the sun and not the moon, which is why it falls on nearly the same date every year. It marks the Sun's entry into Makara, Capricorn, and the start of Uttarayana, the northward course of the Sun. The Mahabharata tells that Bhishma, lying on his bed of arrows, waited for this period before giving up his life, and that story is a big reason Uttarayana is treated as a favourable time for new undertakings.`,
      `The festival has different faces across the country. In Gujarat and Rajasthan the sky fills with kites, and Ahmedabad hosts a large international kite event. In Maharashtra people exchange til-gul sweets with the greeting to speak sweetly, and in Tamil Nadu the same moment becomes Pongal. Punjab marks the eve as Lohri, Assam as Magh Bihu, and Karnataka as Suggi, with the Ellu Bella exchange of sesame and jaggery. In Odisha the day is also honoured with a special Makara Chula dish at the Jagannath temple at Puri, according to local custom.`,
      `Taking a dip in a river is an old custom of the day, and pilgrims gather at the Ganga Sagar confluence in Bengal and at the Magh Mela in Prayagraj. Sesame and jaggery are warming foods that suit the cold season, and giving them away, along with khichdi, blankets or grain, is a form of charity commonly linked to the day. The Mahabharata link also explains why many people avoid weddings in the preceding months and resume them afterward.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why does Makar Sankranti fall on the same date most years?`, a: `Because it is determined by the Sun's movement into Makara and not by a lunar tithi, so the date shifts only slightly over the long term.` },
      { q: `Why are sesame and jaggery eaten?`, a: `They are warming foods suited to winter, and in Maharashtra the sharing of til-gul is a gesture of goodwill.` },
    ],
  },
  'vasant-panchami': {
    about: [
      `Vasant Panchami opens the spring season in the Indian calendar, and the Magha Shukla Panchami is its traditional date. The goddess associated with it is Saraswati, the deity of speech, learning and the arts, usually shown seated on a white lotus with a veena and a book. Yellow, the colour of mustard flowers in bloom across the northern plains, is the colour of the day, and people wear it and prepare yellow rice and sweets.`,
      `The Puranas describe Saraswati as emerging from Brahma's mind, or from the Supreme Goddess, to bring speech and knowledge into creation. In Bengal and Odisha the festival is a major school occasion, with Saraswati Puja held in educational institutions and young children being formally introduced to writing, a ceremony known as haate khori or vidyarambham. The data also mentions children being started on first lessons, and that is a custom you can see in homes in many regions. Pens, instruments and books are placed before the image and left untouched during the worship.`,
      `In Punjab and Haryana the day is also associated with kite-flying and mustard fields, and Sikh communities mark a spring fair at certain places. In some regions the festival has a connection with Kamadeva, the god of love, and with the Holi season, which comes about forty days later. Its description as an auspicious day for beginning anything is a popular reading, and many people take it as permission to start a new lesson, music practice or project without searching for a separate muhurta.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is yellow worn on Vasant Panchami?`, a: `Yellow stands for the blooming mustard fields of early spring and is also associated with Saraswati in popular devotion.` },
      { q: `What is vidyarambham?`, a: `It is the ceremony in which a young child writes the first letters, often with a finger in rice or sand, under the guidance of an elder or teacher.` },
    ],
  },
  'maha-kumbh': {
    about: [
      `The Kumbh Mela is built around a story from the Puranas about the churning of the ocean by gods and demons. When the pot, the kumbha, of nectar emerged, a struggle followed, and drops are said to have fallen at four places: Prayagraj, Haridwar, Ujjain and Nashik. The fairs are held at these four places in rotation, and the timing depends on the positions of Jupiter and the Sun and Moon, which is why astrologers announce the main bathing days far ahead.`,
      `Scale and structure vary. The Ardh Kumbh is held roughly every six years at Prayagraj and Haridwar, and the Purna Kumbh every twelve years at each of the four places. The Maha Kumbh is the very large twelve-year event at Prayagraj, at the confluence of the Ganga, the Yamuna and the mythical Saraswati. The data records the UNESCO recognition of the Kumbh Mela as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2017, which acknowledges it as a living tradition and not only a mass gathering.`,
      `Akharas, the monastic orders of sadhus, lead the procession to the river on the main bathing days, with Naga ascetics bathing first by custom. Alongside the bath, the fair is a place of discourses, debates between teachers, free kitchens and, for many families, reunions. Pilgrims often camp in a temporary city built on the riverbank. Because the crowd on key days is immense, local administrations publish route and safety guidance, and first-time visitors are wise to follow them and avoid peak hours.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is the Kumbh held at four places?`, a: `According to the Puranic story of the nectar pot, drops fell at Prayagraj, Haridwar, Ujjain and Nashik, and the fair rotates among them.` },
      { q: `What is the difference between Kumbh and Maha Kumbh?`, a: `The Kumbh moves among four cities on a roughly three-year cycle, while the Maha Kumbh is the twelve-yearly event held at Prayagraj.` },
    ],
  },
  'akshaya-tritiya': {
    about: [
      `The word akshaya means that which does not diminish, and the day is understood as a time when good deeds, giving and fresh beginnings are said to bear lasting fruit. It falls on the third lunar day of the bright half of Vaishakha. Tradition connects it to a number of events: the beginning of Treta Yuga, the descent of the Ganga in some tellings, the birth of Parashurama according to many accounts, and the day the Pandavas received the Akshaya Patra, the inexhaustible vessel, from the Sun in the Mahabharata story.`,
      `One of the best-loved stories is that of Sudama, the poor friend who brought Krishna a gift of beaten rice and returned to find his home transformed. Jains observe the day as the end of the year-long fast of Rishabhanatha, the first Tirthankara, who broke it with sugarcane juice at Hastinapur, and they call the celebration Varshi Tap. The data mentions that the portals of the Char Dham yatra open around this time, and the temple at Badrinath and the other Himalayan shrines are associated with that opening of the pilgrimage season.`,
      `In many homes the day is marked by buying gold or starting a business, but the older emphasis is on dana, charity, with gifts of water pots, fans, sandals and food at the start of the summer. It is not a day for debt or impulse spending, and thoughtful teachers remind people that the real treasure the festival speaks of is merit and good conduct. Jewellers tend to run offers, and it is wise to treat those as commerce and not as a religious requirement.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Is it compulsory to buy gold on Akshaya Tritiya?`, a: `No. Buying gold is a popular modern custom, whereas the older texts emphasise charity, worship and new beginnings of any kind.` },
      { q: `Why is the day linked to Parashurama and Treta Yuga?`, a: `Puranic traditions place the start of Treta Yuga and, in many accounts, the birth of Parashurama on this tithi, although the accounts differ in detail.` },
    ],
  },
  'narasimha-jayanti': {
    about: [
      `Narasimha, the man-lion, is the fourth avatar of Vishnu in the traditional list of ten. The Bhagavata Purana tells how the demon king Hiranyakashipu received a boon that he could not be killed by man or beast, indoors or outdoors, by day or night, on earth or in the sky, or by any weapon. His son Prahlada remained a steadfast devotee of Vishnu despite repeated attempts on his life. When Hiranyakashipu mocked him and struck a pillar, asking whether Vishnu was in it, Narasimha burst from the pillar at twilight, placed the demon on his lap on the threshold, and tore him apart with his claws, which is how each condition of the boon was technically respected.`,
      `The story is told on the evening of the festival, which is the tithi of the avatar's appearance at dusk. This is why the fast in the data runs until the evening, and the principal worship is held at twilight and not at noon. Narasimha is revered as a fierce protector who is also gentle toward his devotee, and the image of Prahlada seated safely at his side shows that duality. The mantra Ugram Viram Maha-Vishnum is widely chanted on the day.`,
      `The deity has important shrines in Andhra Pradesh at Ahobilam and Simhachalam, at Yadadri in Telangana, and at Melkote in Karnataka. In Andhra and Telangana he is a family deity for many households. Panakam, the jaggery drink, is frequently offered. Exact customs differ between Vaishnava lineages, so it is best to follow your family's or temple's schedule for the evening worship.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why did Vishnu take a half-man, half-lion form?`, a: `The boon of Hiranyakashipu ruled out death by man, animal and many other conditions, and the Narasimha form allowed Vishnu to meet all of them while keeping the promise.` },
      { q: `Why is the worship done at dusk?`, a: `The story says Narasimha appeared and slew Hiranyakashipu at twilight, so evening is the traditional time of the main puja.` },
    ],
  },
  'jagannath-rath-yatra': {
    about: [
      `At Puri in Odisha the deity Jagannath, together with his brother Balabhadra and sister Subhadra, leaves the sanctum once a year and travels in procession on enormous wooden chariots. The three chariots are rebuilt each year from specified timber and are named Nandighosha, Taladhwaja and Darpadalana. The journey goes to the Gundicha temple, about three kilometres away, and the deities return nine days later in the Bahuda Yatra. Odia tradition regards the Yatra as the one day when those who cannot enter the temple, as well as the common person, can see the lord in the open street.`,
      `Jagannath is worshipped in Puri as a form of Vishnu or Krishna, with eyes large and round and limbs unusually short, and the carved wooden images are renewed on a long cycle in a ceremony called Nabakalebara. Before the Yatra, the Snana Yatra bath on the full moon of Jyeshtha is said to leave the deities unwell, and they rest out of sight for a fortnight. Before the chariots leave, the Gajapati king of Puri sweeps the chariot platform with a golden broom in the Chhera Pahanra ritual, symbolising that the lord is above all rank.`,
      `The word juggernaut in English derives from the name Jagannath. Similar processions are held in Ahmedabad, in Bengal at Mahesh and in many places abroad, including those organised by ISKCON in several world cities. Pulling the ropes is considered an act of devotion, and the crowd at Puri is vast, so visitors should plan carefully and follow the instructions of the temple authorities and local police.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Who rides on the three chariots?`, a: `Jagannath, Balabhadra and Subhadra each have their own chariot, with Jagannath's named Nandighosha.` },
      { q: `Why is the Rath Yatra considered unusual?`, a: `Because the deity himself comes out of the temple to meet the people, which is not the norm for most temple images that stay inside the sanctum.` },
    ],
  },
  'nag-panchami': {
    about: [
      `Nag Panchami honours the serpent, which Indian tradition has long treated as both dangerous and sacred. It falls on the fifth day of the bright half of Shravana in most calendars, during the monsoon months when snakes emerge from flooded burrows and the risk of bites rises for farmers. The reverence is practical as well as devotional, since snakes control rodents that threaten stored grain.`,
      `Epic and Puranic stories give the day depth. The Mahabharata recounts the snake sacrifice of King Janamejaya, who set out to destroy all serpents to avenge his father Parikshit, and the sage Astika stopped it, a rescue often remembered on this day. Vishnu rests on Shesha, Shiva wears Vasuki around his neck, and Krishna's subduing of the serpent Kaliya in the Yamuna is another much-told story. In South India worship goes to anthills and snake stones under trees, and in Maharashtra women worship a clay snake and avoid digging the ground that day. The data lists Kaal Sarp dosha as a common reason for the rites, but this is an astrological idea of later origin, so devotees who are unsure should consult their own family tradition.`,
      `Milk is poured over the image or the anthill as a devotional offering, though herpetologists point out that snakes cannot digest milk and that live snakes should not be handled or fed. A clay or silver image, flowers, turmeric and rice make an adequate offering. Kerala has its own Nagaraja temple at Mannarasala, where snake worship is a long family tradition.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Is it harmful to give milk to a real snake?`, a: `Yes. Snakes do not drink milk and forcing it on them can injure them, so offering milk to an image or a symbolic anthill is the better custom.` },
      { q: `Who is Astika in the Nag Panchami story?`, a: `Astika is the sage who, according to the Mahabharata, persuaded King Janamejaya to end his sacrifice of serpents.` },
    ],
  },
  'raksha-bandhan': {
    about: [
      `Raksha Bandhan means the bond of protection. A sister ties a thread called a rakhi on her brother's wrist, applies a mark on his forehead and offers sweets, and the brother promises to look after her and gives a gift. The day is placed on the Shravana full moon, a time that also marks the changing of the sacred thread among Brahmin communities and the festival of Narali Purnima among fishing communities on the western coast.`,
      `The data refers to the story of Indra and Sachi in the Bhavishya Purana, in which Indra's wife tied a protective thread on his wrist before a battle with the demons. The thread there is a talisman and not a family symbol. Another story is that of Draupadi tearing a strip of her sari to bandage Krishna's wounded finger, after which he vowed to protect her, which is often retold. There is also the legend of Queen Karnavati of Chittor sending a rakhi to the Mughal emperor Humayun seeking help, which is a historical tradition told in Rajasthan, though historians debate its details.`,
      `The relationship need not be by blood. Women tie rakhis to cousins, neighbours, soldiers and friends, and in Maharashtra and Gujarat the day mixes with sea-worship rites. In Bengal and Odisha the nearby full moon is Jhulan Purnima, when Radha and Krishna are swung on a swing. Brothers who live far away often receive rakhis by post, and sisters can plan in advance for deliveries. Eco-friendly rakhis made from seeds or cotton are a modern alternative to plastic.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Can a rakhi be tied to someone who is not a brother?`, a: `Yes. Many people tie rakhis to cousins, friends, neighbours or soldiers as a sign of care and protection.` },
      { q: `Is there a stated time to tie the rakhi?`, a: `Traditional calendars warn against a period called Bhadra and advise tying outside it, so many families check the local panchang for the right window.` },
    ],
  },
  'onam': {
    about: [
      `Onam is the great festival of Kerala, rooted in the story of Mahabali, the generous asura king whose reign is remembered as an age of equality and plenty. The Bhagavata Purana tells how Vishnu came as the dwarf Vamana and asked Mahabali for three paces of land. The second pace covered the heavens and the earth, and Mahabali offered his own head for the third, so Vamana sent him to rule the nether world of Sutala. Because of his love for his subjects, he is allowed to visit them once a year, and Onam is the occasion of that visit.`,
      `Celebrations run for ten days, with Atham at the start and Thiruvonam as the peak, and the festival marks the harvest month of Chingam in the Malayalam calendar. A pookalam, a floral carpet, is laid in the courtyard and enlarged each day. The grand sadya is served on banana leaf with a long list of vegetarian dishes. Pulikali tiger dances in Thrissur, Vallam Kali snake-boat races at Aranmula and Alappuzha, and Kaikottikali, a women's clapping dance, belong to the season. Onam is a state festival in Kerala, and it is shared across communities, which is the spirit of the story.`,
      `Temples dedicated to Vamana, such as Thrikkakara near Kochi, hold special observances. Mahabali, in homes, is welcomed with clay pyramids known as Onathappan. Because the festival follows the Malayalam solar calendar and the Thiruvonam star, its Gregorian date shifts from year to year, and Malayali communities abroad plan their own celebrations over weeks.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Who is Mahabali in the Onam story?`, a: `He is the asura king of the Puranas, remembered for his generosity, who was sent to the netherworld by Vamana but is believed to visit his people once a year.` },
      { q: `What is a pookalam?`, a: `A pookalam is a floral design laid on the ground at the entrance of the home, enlarged day by day through the festival.` },
    ],
  },
  'pitru-paksha': {
    about: [
      `Pitru Paksha is the fortnight devoted to the pitris, the departed ancestors, and the tradition holds that the dead are remembered with water, food and prayer in this period. It follows the full moon of Bhadrapada and runs through the dark half, and each day corresponds to a tithi so that one performs the shraddha on the tithi when the person died. The data calls it a sixteen-day period, which fits the count of the tithis. The final new moon, Sarva Pitru Amavasya, serves for those whose death date is unknown.`,
      `The rites centre on tarpana, a libation of water with sesame and barley, and pinda, balls of cooked rice offered to the departed. The Garuda Purana and the Mahabharata discuss duties owed to ancestors, and the Mahabharata story of Karna, who is said to have returned to earth for a fortnight to give the food he had never offered in life, is widely quoted as the origin of the practice. Gaya in Bihar is the most famous place for pinda dana, and Prayagraj, Haridwar, Varanasi, Pushkar and Nashik are also much used. Many families perform the rites at home with a priest and feed Brahmins, cows, crows and dogs.`,
      `The period is generally not used for weddings, house entry or new purchases, which are postponed until Navaratri. The idea behind this is more about the mood of remembrance than any danger. Communities in Bengal call the same season Mahalaya and in Maharashtra the practice is called Pitr Paksha as well. A family without access to a pilgrimage place can still offer water and food at home, and many teachers say that the sincerity of the offering is the core of it.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is Sarva Pitru Amavasya?`, a: `It is the last day of the fortnight, on which anyone may perform shraddha for all ancestors, especially when the death date is not known.` },
      { q: `Why are crows fed during Pitru Paksha?`, a: `In popular belief crows carry offerings to ancestors, so a portion of the shraddha meal is set aside for them.` },
    ],
  },
  'dussehra': {
    about: [
      `Dussehra, also called Vijayadashami, falls on the tenth day after the start of autumn Navaratri and is the festival of victory. Two great stories meet on it. In the Ramayana tradition Rama defeats the ten-headed Ravana, and in the Devi tradition the Goddess finishes her battle against Mahishasura. The name Dussehra is generally explained as from dasha-hara, the removal of ten, a reference to Ravana's ten heads, but folk explanations differ.`,
      `The day takes distinct local forms. In north India, Ramlila performances of the Rama story over the preceding days close with the burning of large effigies of Ravana, Kumbhakarna and Meghanada at dusk, with the Delhi and Ramnagar Ramlila traditions among the oldest. The Kullu festival in Himachal gathers local village deities in a procession, while Mysuru's Dasara has royal origins connected to the Vijayanagara rulers and is held in the palace city. In Bengal, Vijayadashami is the day of the farewell to the Goddess with the immersion of the images, and in Maharashtra and Karnataka people exchange leaves of the apta or shami tree as symbolic gold.`,
      `The Mahabharata gives the shami tree another meaning: the Pandavas hid their weapons in one during their last year of exile and recovered them on this day. That explains the Shastra Puja, the worship of tools and weapons, and the custom of starting new learning on the day. Farmers, craftspeople and drivers often worship their own implements. Because large fireworks and effigies affect local air quality, many town committees now keep them modest.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is the shami tree worshipped on Dussehra?`, a: `According to the Mahabharata the Pandavas hid their weapons in a shami tree and retrieved them on this day, so the tree and weapons are honoured.` },
      { q: `Does Dussehra celebrate only Rama's victory?`, a: `No. In many regions, especially Bengal, it marks the Goddess's victory over Mahishasura and the close of Durga Puja.` },
    ],
  },
  'karwa-chauth': {
    about: [
      `Karwa Chauth is a one-day fast kept by married women in north and north-western India, placed on the fourth day of the dark half of Kartika. A karva is a small earthen pot, and the pots are part of the ritual, being filled with grain or sweets and exchanged. Women usually wake before dawn for a pre-dawn meal called sargi, often prepared by the mother-in-law, and then do not eat or drink until the moon rises.`,
      `The best-known story is that of Veeravati, a princess who broke her fast early after her brothers deceived her with a false moonrise, and who then lost her husband until she repeated the fast with full rigour. The Mahabharata tradition says that Draupadi, facing difficulty, was advised by Krishna to take a similar fast. Another view, advanced by some scholars, is that the custom began as friendship between women at marriage in the farming belt, when brides married far away adopted a friend called a karva sakhi. These explanations are not fully reconciled, so the origin is best described as plural.`,
      `In the evening the women assemble to hear the story, pass thalis around in a circle, and then see the moon through a sieve before looking at the husband and receiving the first sip. Women in Gujarat, Rajasthan, Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh and Delhi all observe it, though practices differ. The fast without water can be difficult, and traditional teachers accept modifications for those who are pregnant, unwell or elderly. Some husbands now fast alongside their wives.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is sargi?`, a: `Sargi is the pre-dawn meal eaten before the Karwa Chauth fast begins, usually given by the husband's mother and consisting of fruit, sweets and simple dishes.` },
      { q: `Why is the moon seen through a sieve?`, a: `It is a traditional practice, usually explained as a gentle way of viewing the moon before turning to the husband to break the fast.` },
    ],
  },
  'dhanteras': {
    about: [
      `Dhanteras opens the Diwali season on the thirteenth day of the dark half of Kartika, and the name joins dhana, wealth, with teras, the thirteenth. Older sources give the day a medical emphasis as well. It is celebrated as the day Dhanvantari, the physician of the gods, emerged from the churning of the ocean carrying a pot of amrita, and this is why Ayurveda practitioners mark it as Dhanvantari Jayanti.`,
      `There is also a well-known folk tale about the sixteen-year-old son of King Hima, who was fated to die of snakebite on the fourth night of his marriage. His young wife heaped her ornaments and coins at the door and sang through the night, and the dazzle and the music kept Yama, who came as a serpent, from entering. That is the reason lamps are lit toward the south in the evening in many homes, as a Yama deepam, to offer light for the lord of death. The custom is described in regional sources, not in a single scripture.`,
      `Buying a new utensil, a coin or a small piece of jewellery is the best-known custom, as it is thought to bring good fortune to the year ahead, but the intent of the day is also to clean the home and take stock. Businesses clean their accounts and tools. Brooms, which sweep out the old, are sold in markets in many northern towns. Gold is a modern emphasis, and any purchase within one's budget serves the purpose. The evening puja to Lakshmi, Kubera and Dhanvantari is simple and brief.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why are utensils bought on Dhanteras?`, a: `Dhanvantari is said to have appeared holding a pot of nectar, so a new vessel is taken as a symbol of that pot and of good fortune.` },
      { q: `What is the Yama deepam?`, a: `It is a lamp lit in the evening, placed outside the house and facing south, as a prayer to Yama for protection from untimely death.` },
    ],
  },
  'govardhan-puja': {
    about: [
      `Govardhan Puja is the day after the main Diwali night, and it commemorates one of the most loved episodes of the Bhagavata Purana. The people of Vraj used to worship Indra for rain, and young Krishna suggested instead that they honour Govardhan hill, which fed their cows and gave them pasture. Angered, Indra sent storms, and Krishna lifted the hill on the little finger of one hand to shelter the people and cattle for seven days, after which Indra acknowledged him.`,
      `The festival is also called Annakut, which means a mountain of grain, and in many temples a heap of cooked rice and a large number of dishes is arranged to represent the hill. The data refers to a bhog of fifty-six dishes, chappan bhog, as an offering to Krishna, which is particularly associated with Vaishnava temples such as those at Nathdwara. Cattle are washed, decorated and fed, and in some villages a cow-dung hill is modelled in the courtyard and circled. Gujarat observes the day as the beginning of the new Vikram year, and in Maharashtra it appears as Bali Pratipada, remembering the return of King Bali.`,
      `Pilgrims in Braj walk the Govardhan parikrama, a circuit of around twenty-one kilometres on foot, and the data lists large numbers on this day. The theme underlying the story is care for the natural world that sustains a community, and the tradition speaks of gratitude to the land, hill and cattle. For urban families the observance can be as simple as preparing a sweet dish, offering it with a prayer, and sharing it with neighbours.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is Annakut?`, a: `Annakut means a mountain of food, and temples build a large arrangement of cooked dishes to represent the Govardhan hill on this day.` },
      { q: `Why did Krishna lift Govardhan?`, a: `According to the Bhagavata Purana he lifted it to shelter the people of Vraj from the storm that Indra sent after the villagers stopped worshipping him.` },
    ],
  },
  'bhai-dooj': {
    about: [
      `Bhai Dooj, also known as Bhau Beej, Bhai Tika and Bhratri Dwitiya, belongs to the second day of the bright half of Kartika, which is the day after Govardhan Puja. The data points to its source in the legend of Yama and his sister Yamuna. Yama, the god of death, visited Yamuna after a long absence, and she welcomed him with a tilak, a garland and a meal. Pleased, he promised that a brother who receives such hospitality from his sister on this day would be freed from the fear of untimely death.`,
      `Because of that story, there is a custom in parts of north India of bathing in the Yamuna at Mathura on this day, and the Vishram Ghat is the place usually associated with it. Customs differ elsewhere. In Maharashtra it is Bhau Beej and the sister performs an aarti with a small lamp. In Bengal the equivalent is Bhai Phonta, in which a sister marks her brother's forehead with sandal paste and wishes him a long life while fasting until she has done so. In Nepal the Newar community observes a similar festival named Kija Puja.`,
      `The festival differs from Raksha Bandhan in that the sister blesses the brother, and the brother, after the tilak, gives a gift. It brings the five-day Diwali season to a close and is often an excuse for the family to gather. Married sisters traditionally invite their brothers for a meal, and the brother travels to her home. If a sister lives far away, a tilak can be applied by a cousin or sent as a message of good wishes, and the spirit of the day still holds.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `How is Bhai Dooj different from Raksha Bandhan?`, a: `On Raksha Bandhan the sister ties a thread and the brother promises protection, whereas on Bhai Dooj she applies a tilak and prays for his long life.` },
      { q: `Which tithi does Bhai Dooj follow?`, a: `It follows the second lunar day of the bright half of Kartika, two days after the main Diwali night.` },
    ],
  },
  'tulsi-vivah': {
    about: [
      `Tulsi Vivah is the ritual wedding of the tulsi plant to Vishnu, who is represented by a Shaligram stone or an image of Krishna. It is held on a date in the bright half of Kartika, usually from the Ekadashi up to the full moon, and by it the four-month period of Chaturmas is considered concluded and the wedding season begins. The plant stands in a decorated pot or a masonry platform in the courtyard, dressed as a bride with a red cloth, bangles and flowers.`,
      `The Padma Purana tells the story behind it. Tulsi was Vrinda, the devoted wife of the demon Jalandhara, and her chastity made him invincible. When Vishnu took Jalandhara's form to break her vow, he was cursed to become a stone, which is why the Shaligram is a black stone, and Vrinda gave up her life and was reborn as the sacred plant. Vishnu in turn promised to marry her in that form. The tale has more than one telling, and some regions emphasise the devotion of Vrinda and others the cursing of Vishnu.`,
      `In households the marriage is performed with a mandap of sugarcane stalks, a groom's procession in some places, and mangalashtaka verses. In Maharashtra and Gujarat the day is observed with particular care, and the first wedding invitations of the season often follow. The data notes that giving Tulsi in marriage is spoken of as equal to kanyadana, which is a traditional reading found in the Puranic texts. Tulsi is a living plant, so watering and tending it well is the real long-term form of the worship.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Who is Tulsi in the Puranas?`, a: `In the Padma Purana she is Vrinda, the devoted wife of the demon Jalandhara, who was reborn as the tulsi plant and is wed to Vishnu.` },
      { q: `What is a Shaligram?`, a: `A Shaligram is a dark fossil stone from the Gandaki river in Nepal, worshipped as a natural form of Vishnu.` },
    ],
  },
  'kartik-purnima': {
    about: [
      `The full moon of Kartika is one of the most layered days in the Hindu year and is regarded as the high point of a month of devotion. Pilgrims take the Kartika snan, a dawn bath in a sacred river, through the month, and the final full moon is the occasion for the largest gatherings. The data gives its main name as Dev Diwali, the festival of the lamps of the gods, and says that Shiva destroyed the demon Tripurasura on this day, which is why it is also called Tripuri Purnima.`,
      `At Varanasi, the ghats along the Ganga are lined with countless clay lamps from Assi to Rajghat, and the evening Ganga aarti is among the most photographed sights. In Pushkar in Rajasthan a pilgrim fair is held around the lake that is believed to be associated with Brahma, and in Odisha Bali Yatra and the Boita Bandana float little paper boats on water in memory of ancient sea voyages to Southeast Asia. In Tamil Nadu this is also the season of Karthigai, and in Matsya Purana tradition the avatar of Matsya, the fish, is connected with the day.`,
      `Sikhs celebrate the birth of Guru Nanak on the same full moon, as noted in the data, and Jains in Palitana make a pilgrimage on the day. The shared date shows how the full moon is an anchor for several traditions. Giving lamps to a river, donating food and wearing simple clothes are widely practised, and the Matsya Purana and others speak of the merit of charity in this month. Floating leaf boats, not plastic, protects the river itself.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is it called Dev Diwali?`, a: `Tradition says the gods descend to bathe in the Ganga on this night, and Varanasi lights its ghats with lamps in their honour.` },
      { q: `Why is it also called Tripuri Purnima?`, a: `Because Shiva is said to have destroyed the three demon cities of Tripurasura on this day, according to Puranic accounts.` },
    ],
  },
  'vivah-panchami': {
    about: [
      `Vivah Panchami commemorates the wedding of Rama and Sita, held according to the Ramcharitmanas in the city of Janakpur, the capital of Mithila in what is now Nepal. The story begins with the swayamvara of Sita, where the great bow of Shiva was placed before the assembled kings, and only Rama could lift and string it. After the bow broke, King Janaka offered his daughter, and the marriage of all four brothers to the four princesses of Mithila was solemnised. The festival falls on the fifth day of the bright half of Margashirsha.`,
      `Janakpur's Janaki Mandir stages a grand re-enactment each year, with a procession of Rama's party arriving from Ayodhya and ritual performances of the wedding. Ayodhya also holds celebrations, and Ram Katha is recited in many temples across India. In Maithili tradition, folk songs of banter between the families, called gaali, accompany the ritual.`,
      `A curious point recorded in the data is that in some regions people avoid choosing this day for real weddings. The reason given is that the life of Sita after the wedding included exile, separation and difficulty, and families prefer not to invite that memory to a new household. This is a regional belief, particularly in Mithila, and not a rule followed by all communities, so families should rely on their own priest and calendar. Others treat the day as a good time for fixing an engagement or beginning the search for a match. Reading the Balakanda of the Ramayana or the wedding chapters of the Manas is a quiet way to mark it.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Where was the wedding of Rama and Sita held?`, a: `According to the Ramcharitmanas it took place at Janakpur in Mithila, the kingdom of Sita's father Janaka.` },
      { q: `Why do some people avoid weddings on Vivah Panchami?`, a: `In parts of Mithila it is thought that Sita's later hardships make the day unsuitable for a real wedding, though other regions do not share the view.` },
    ],
  },
  'mokshada-ekadashi': {
    about: [
      `Among the twenty-four Ekadashis of the year, Mokshada Ekadashi, the one that gives liberation, falls in the bright half of Margashirsha. The Brahmanda Purana relates a story told by Krishna to Yudhishthira about King Vaikhanasa of Champaka, who dreamed that his father was suffering in hell. A sage told him that his father could be relieved if the king observed this Ekadashi and offered its merit to him. The king did so, and his father is said to have attained a better state.`,
      `That is why the fast is connected with the welfare of ancestors, a theme the data repeats. The day also bears a second identity, since it is the one on which tradition says Krishna spoke the Bhagavad Gita to Arjuna at Kurukshetra, and it is celebrated as Gita Jayanti. In Vaishnava temples the Gita is read from morning, and a day of chanting verses is a popular way to observe it. The Vishnu Sahasranama is also recited.`,
      `Ekadashi fasting follows a standard pattern across Vaishnava traditions: a lighter meal on the preceding day, a fast on Ekadashi without grain, and the breaking of the fast, called parana, on the morning of Dwadashi within the stated window. People who cannot fast fully often take fruit, milk or a single satvic meal. Anyone with a health condition should adjust the fast without guilt. Temples and ISKCON centres frequently arrange community recitations. The data records that Gitas are distributed in large numbers around the date, and giving a book is regarded as a merit in itself.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What does Mokshada mean?`, a: `Mokshada means the giver of moksha, or liberation, and the name reflects the traditional view that this Ekadashi benefits the observer and ancestors.` },
      { q: `Is Mokshada Ekadashi the same as Gita Jayanti?`, a: `Tradition links the two, since the Gita is said to have been taught on the Margashirsha Shukla Ekadashi, so the dates are usually the same.` },
    ],
  },
  'gudi-padwa': {
    about: [
      `Gudi Padwa opens the new year for Marathi and Konkani households on the first day of the bright half of Chaitra. The word padwa comes from pratipada, the first lunar day, and gudi is the banner raised outside the door. It is typically a bamboo staff dressed with a bright silk cloth, a garland of sugar crystals, neem and mango leaves, and an upturned copper or silver pot on top. It stands where the morning sun can reach it and is taken down at sunset.`,
      `Explanations of the gudi differ. One view takes it as a flag of Brahma, who created the world on this day in the Puranic account. Another connects it to Rama's return to Ayodhya, with the staff as the banner of homecoming. A popular historical reading ties it to the victory of Shalivahana over the Shakas, which is the origin of the Shaka era, and Maratha tradition associates it with the military triumphs of Shivaji. These readings coexist, and each household may emphasise one.`,
      `The day begins with an oil bath and the eating of a bitter-sweet mixture of neem leaves, jaggery and tamarind, a taste of what the year may hold. Shrikhand and puran poli are the usual festive foods. The Panchang is read for the year ahead. The same tithi marks Ugadi in the Deccan and Navreh among Kashmiri Pandits, and the nine days that follow are Chaitra Navaratri. The data lists the date as the same as Ugadi, and this is typical of a shared lunar calendar with regional names.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is a pot placed upside down on the gudi?`, a: `It is generally explained as a sign of victory or auspiciousness, and in some readings the pot represents Brahma's creation.` },
      { q: `Why is neem eaten on this day?`, a: `The bitter neem with sweet jaggery is a reminder that the year will bring both difficulty and joy, and it is also a seasonal health custom.` },
    ],
  },
  'ugadi': {
    about: [
      `Ugadi, from the Sanskrit yuga and adi, the beginning of an age, is the new year of Andhra Pradesh, Telangana and Karnataka. It falls on the first day of the bright half of Chaitra, the same lunar day on which Maharashtra celebrates Gudi Padwa. Brahma is said in the Puranic account to have begun creation on this day, and the Matsya Purana is sometimes cited for it. The date also marks the start of the Shalivahana Shaka era in much of the Deccan.`,
      `The special food is Ugadi Pachadi, a mixture in which neem flowers, raw mango, jaggery, tamarind, salt and chilli are blended. In Telugu tradition the six tastes are read as a lesson that life contains many flavours, and that the year ahead will contain a mix of experiences. In Karnataka the preparation is called bevu-bella and consists of neem and jaggery. Mango leaf garlands hang at doors, and the household takes an oil bath before dawn. Bobbatlu, or holige, a sweet flat bread, is the festive dish.`,
      `Another central custom is Panchanga Sravanam, the listening to the almanac. A priest or elder reads the predictions of the year, and the temple or village gathers to hear them. This is both a religious ceremony and a social one. Poets in Telugu and Kannada communities also hold kavi sammelanas, meetings of poets, as part of the occasion. Because the festival is grounded in the spring, with mango blossom and new leaves, it reads as a renewal of nature as well as of the calendar. Gentle resolutions for the new year are a modern but fitting way to take part.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What are the six tastes in Ugadi Pachadi?`, a: `Sweet from jaggery, sour from tamarind, salty from salt, bitter from neem, pungent from chilli and tangy from raw mango, representing varied experiences of life.` },
      { q: `What is Panchanga Sravanam?`, a: `It is the traditional reading of the year's almanac, with its predictions, by a priest or scholar on Ugadi.` },
    ],
  },
  'baisakhi': {
    about: [
      `Baisakhi, also written Vaisakhi, falls when the Sun enters Aries, which makes it a solar and not a lunar festival and therefore close to the same date each year. In Punjab the day marks the ripening of the rabi wheat and the start of harvest, and fields, fairs and folk dances take over. In the same period, other regions celebrate their own new year: Puthandu in Tamil Nadu, Vishu in Kerala, Bihu in Assam and Pohela Boishakh in Bengal, a shared moment in a solar calendar.`,
      `For Sikhs the day has a particular importance, which the data records. In 1699 Guru Gobind Singh gathered the community at Anandpur Sahib, called for five volunteers who became the Panj Pyare, and initiated them with amrit, founding the Khalsa. Gurdwaras hold special services and large processions, called nagar kirtan, led by the Panj Pyare. Anandpur Sahib and Talwandi Sabo are the main centres, and Amritsar's Golden Temple sees large crowds. The data presents the day within the Hindu calendar, and it is worth stating clearly that the Khalsa foundation is a Sikh tradition, shared by neighbours as a regional celebration.`,
      `For Hindu families, Baisakhi is also an occasion for a river bath, temple visits and thanksgiving for the harvest. In Himachal, Haryana and the hills of Uttarakhand fairs and local deity processions are held. In Punjab the energetic dances bhangra and giddha are strongly associated with the season, and farmers visit the fields and offer the first of the grain with thanks before sale. The harvest season is hard work, so the festival is also a time of rest and meals shared with neighbours.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why does Baisakhi fall on nearly the same date each year?`, a: `It follows the Sun's entry into Aries, a solar event, and not a lunar tithi.` },
      { q: `What happened at Anandpur Sahib in 1699?`, a: `Guru Gobind Singh created the Khalsa, initiating the five beloved ones, the Panj Pyare, with amrit, an event commemorated by Sikhs.` },
    ],
  },
  'pongal': {
    about: [
      `Pongal is the harvest thanksgiving of Tamil Nadu, and its name means to boil over. Rice is cooked with milk and jaggery in a new clay pot, often outdoors, until it foams up and spills, at which point the household shouts Pongalo Pongal. The spilling is taken as a sign of abundance for the year ahead. The festival is held in the Tamil month of Thai, which begins with the Sun's entry into Makara, the same moment as Makar Sankranti.`,
      `The four days have distinct characters. Bhogi, the first, is a day for discarding old things and lighting a fire of unneeded household items, in some places with drumming. Thai Pongal, the second, is the central day, with worship of Surya and the cooking of the pongal. Mattu Pongal, the third, honours cattle, who are bathed, adorned with garlands and fed. In rural areas jallikattu, the bull-taming sport, is held, but it has been controversial and has seen legal debate. Kaanum Pongal, the fourth, is for visits to relatives and outings, with offerings of food for birds.`,
      `Sugarcane stalks, turmeric plants tied to the pot, and kolam designs drawn with rice flour decorate the household. Tamil Sangam literature refers to harvest festivals in the month of Thai, and the tradition of Thai Nombu and the Tamil expression Thai piranthal vazhi pirakkum, which hopes that the month of Thai will open the way, is a popular saying. Tamil communities in Sri Lanka, Malaysia, Singapore and elsewhere also celebrate the festival with local variations.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why must the pot boil over?`, a: `Overflowing milk and rice is treated as a sign of prosperity, and the household cheers at the moment it happens.` },
      { q: `What is Bhogi?`, a: `It is the first day of Pongal, devoted to clearing out old belongings and marking the end of the old year.` },
    ],
  },
  'lohri': {
    about: [
      `Lohri is lit in the cold of mid-winter, in the evening before Makar Sankranti, and it belongs especially to Punjab, Haryana and Himachal. A bonfire is built in the courtyard or street, and as it blazes, people walk around it, throw in sesame, jaggery, popcorn and peanuts, and sing. The fire is a form of Agni, who receives the offering, and the ritual celebrates the end of the coldest part of the year as the days start to lengthen.`,
      `The lyrics sung are tied to the folk hero Dulla Bhatti, who is remembered in Punjabi ballads as a rebel of Akbar's reign who rescued girls from being sold and arranged their weddings. The song Sundar Mundriye is the best known, and children go door to door singing it and collecting small gifts of sweets and money. The data calls him a Punjabi Robin Hood, which is a popular description. Historians place the details of his life within Mughal-period Punjab, but the ballad is mostly folk memory.`,
      `Lohri is also a family milestone festival: a newly married couple's first Lohri and the first Lohri of a newborn are celebrated with extra festivity. The foods of the season include gajak, rewari, sarson da saag and makki di roti. As farmers see the winter wheat crop standing in the fields, there is a sense of waiting and hope that the harvest at Baisakhi will be good. Today the bonfire is often held in apartment compounds, and a modest fire, kept at a safe distance, with attention to local rules, is sufficient.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Who is Dulla Bhatti?`, a: `A Punjabi folk figure in Mughal times celebrated in Lohri songs for rescuing girls and arranging their marriages.` },
      { q: `What is thrown into the Lohri fire?`, a: `People offer sesame seeds, jaggery, rewari, popcorn and peanuts as a symbolic offering to Agni.` },
    ],
  },
  'durga-puja': {
    about: [
      `In Bengal and across the Bengali diaspora, Durga Puja is less a ritual than a season. The Goddess is welcomed as a daughter returning from Kailash to her parental home with her four children, Lakshmi, Saraswati, Ganesha and Kartikeya, and the festival is the family reunion that Bengali culture holds most dear. The scriptural frame is the Devi Mahatmya, which tells of Durga overcoming Mahishasura, but the emotional tone is that of homecoming and farewell.`,
      `The sequence begins with Mahalaya, when the Goddess is invoked, and the eyes of the image are painted in a ritual called chokkhu daan. The main days are Shashthi, Saptami, Ashtami and Navami, followed by Vijayadashami and the immersion. On Ashtami and Navami the Sandhi Puja is performed at the point when the two tithis meet, and in traditional households a hundred and eight lamps are lit. The data points to the artisans of Kumartuli in Kolkata, who shape the clay images, and to the Bonedi Bari household pujas, some of which have continued for centuries according to their families. The earliest pujas in the rich homes of Bengal are often placed in the eighteenth century, though family accounts vary.`,
      `Community or barowari pujas, with their theme-based pandals, are a more recent development, and they turn neighbourhoods into open-air exhibitions. UNESCO inscribed the Durga Puja of Kolkata on its list of intangible heritage in 2021, as the data mentions. The final day has the sindoor khela, in which married women smear vermilion on one another and on the Goddess, before the procession to the river. Visiting pandals is part of the pleasure, and comfortable footwear, patience with crowds and a respect for pandal safety instructions are good advice.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is Sandhi Puja?`, a: `It is a special worship held at the junction of Ashtami and Navami, traditionally the moment when Durga is said to have slain the demon Chanda and Munda.` },
      { q: `Why are the images made of clay?`, a: `Clay from the Ganga and other materials are customary in Bengal, and the image is immersed at the end to return to the water.` },
    ],
  },
  'vishu': {
    about: [
      `Vishu is the Malayali new year, celebrated on the first of Medam when the Sun enters Aries, and its central idea is the first thing a person sees. This is the Vishukkani, an arrangement made on the night before in the prayer room. A bell-metal vessel called an uruli holds raw rice, golden konna flowers, fruit, vegetables, a metal mirror, gold ornaments, and a cloth, and beside it stands a lamp and an image of Krishna. On waking, family members are led there with eyes closed so that the first sight of the year is an auspicious one.`,
      `The tradition of kani is bound up with the belief that what greets the eye at the start of the year influences the year ahead. The custom is mainly cultural, and families keep it as a sign of hope and abundance. After the viewing, elders hand out coins known as Vishukkaineettam to children and juniors. Firecrackers, a festive sadya, and a visit to a temple such as Guruvayur or Sabarimala complete the day, with Guruvayur known for its pre-dawn kani darshan of the deity.`,
      `A legend sometimes told in Kerala connects the day to Krishna's defeat of Narakasura, and another to the festival's origin as an agricultural marker for the planting season. These are popular accounts, and the day itself is better understood as a seasonal turning. It shares its astronomical moment with Baisakhi, Puthandu and Bihu. The konna blossoms, called the golden shower, flower in April across Kerala, so the yellow colour is a natural part of the setting. Those living away from Kerala often arrange a small kani at home with whatever ingredients are available.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What goes into a Vishukkani?`, a: `Typically rice, konna flowers, fruit, vegetables, a mirror, gold, a cloth and a lamp with an image of Krishna, though households vary.` },
      { q: `What is Vishukkaineettam?`, a: `It is the gift of coins or money from elders to children and younger relatives after the kani viewing.` },
    ],
  },
  'bonalu': {
    about: [
      `Bonalu is a festival of the Mother Goddess in Telangana, held during the month of Ashada, and it takes a distinctly folk form. Women prepare a bonam, an offering of cooked rice with jaggery, milk and curd in a clay pot decorated with turmeric, vermilion and neem leaves, and place a lamp on top. Carrying the pot on the head, they walk in procession to the Goddess, known in different forms such as Mahankali, Pochamma, Yellamma and Maisamma. Men accompany the procession with drums, and the potharaju, a figure in bright cloth and bells, dances in front.`,
      `The festival is concentrated in Hyderabad and Secunderabad, with the Golconda Fort Jagadamba temple opening the cycle, then the Ujjaini Mahankali temple at Secunderabad and the Lal Darwaza temple in the old city. The data records an origin in a plague outbreak in the early nineteenth century, when soldiers in a regiment prayed to the Goddess at Ujjain and, on return, installed a shrine at Secunderabad. This is the local historical account, and other accounts speak of older village traditions of Mother Goddess worship that precede it. The Telangana government declared it the state festival in 2014.`,
      `On the Monday after the main Sunday at Secunderabad, a woman called a matangi gives the Rangam, a prediction for the coming year, standing on a clay pot. The ritual of the Ghatam, a decorated pot carried in procession, closes the season. Because the monsoon season brings disease, the Goddess is regarded as a protector of village and community health, and the offering is a thanksgiving. The festival has spread among Telangana communities abroad, and it remains a women-led devotional tradition.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is a bonam?`, a: `It is the pot of cooked rice with milk, jaggery and curd, decorated and carried on the head as an offering to the Goddess.` },
      { q: `Who gives the Rangam?`, a: `A woman, in a ritual at the Ujjaini Mahankali temple, who stands on an earthen pot and gives a prophecy for the coming year.` },
    ],
  },
  'mahalaya-amavasya': {
    about: [
      `Mahalaya Amavasya is the hinge between two moods. On this new moon the fortnight of Pitru Paksha, devoted to the dead, ends, and the days of the Goddess begin. The data calls it the universal shraddha day, Sarva Pitru Amavasya, on which offerings may be made for any ancestor, including those whose date of death is not remembered. In the Bengal tradition, the same day is Mahalaya, the invocation of Durga, and the countdown to Durga Puja starts.`,
      `The rites are simple in outline. Descendants offer water mixed with black sesame, called tarpana, and cooked rice balls, called pinda, often on a riverbank or at a pond, to their paternal and maternal forebears. The Mahabharata story of Karna, mentioned in the data, tells how the donor of gold arrived in heaven to find only gold on offer and was allowed to return to earth to give food to the departed. It is told as a reason for the days of Pitru Paksha. Tradition-minded families do the rite with a priest, and others offer water and food at home.`,
      `In Bengal, many people wake in the dark hours to listen to Mahishasuramardini, a radio programme of Chandi verses and songs that was first aired on All India Radio in 1931, and its voice, led by Birendra Krishna Bhadra, is still played every year. The data describes it as a nearly century-long ritual, and for many listeners it is the true start of the festive season. At Gaya, Varanasi and along the Ganga, Hooghly, and other rivers, crowds gather before dawn. In 2026, the data places the day on 9 October.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is the difference between Mahalaya and Pitru Paksha?`, a: `Pitru Paksha is the whole fortnight of ancestor rites, and Mahalaya Amavasya is its final new moon, which is also the start of the Goddess's season in Bengal.` },
      { q: `Why do Bengalis listen to Mahishasuramardini?`, a: `The pre-dawn radio programme has become a tradition since 1931, and its recitation of the Chandi invokes the Goddess at the start of the festive season.` },
    ],
  },
  'thaipusam': {
    about: [
      `Thaipusam is the festival of Murugan, the Tamil god of war and wisdom, held when the star Pusam is in the ascendant in the month of Thai. It recalls the moment when Parvati gave her son a vel, a spear, with which he overcame the demon Surapadma, as told in the Skanda Purana and the Tamil Kanda Puranam. The vel is thus both a weapon and an emblem of knowledge, and it is carried or displayed in the processions.`,
      `Its most striking feature is the kavadi, a decorated frame, usually carried on the shoulders, that symbolises a burden carried as a vow. Devotees prepare for weeks by fasting, abstaining and praying, and many carry pots of milk, called paal kudam, to the temple for the abhishekam of the deity. Some undertake extreme acts of penance such as piercing the cheeks and tongue with a small vel, which is part of the folk tradition and is performed under the guidance of experienced practitioners. This is not expected of ordinary devotees and is not considered the only form of worship.`,
      `In Tamil Nadu the six famous abodes of Murugan, the Aarupadai Veedu, including Palani, are the centres of the festival. Outside India the festival is especially visible in Malaysia, where the Batu Caves, with their steep stairway, gather huge crowds, and in Singapore, where a procession runs between two temples. Penang has its own large celebrations. The festival is a public holiday in Malaysia and Singapore. For spectators, the courtesy is to keep a respectful distance, to refrain from interrupting the processions, and to follow the temple's guidance on photography and crowd management.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is a kavadi?`, a: `A kavadi is a decorated frame or burden carried to a Murugan temple as an act of vow or thanksgiving.` },
      { q: `Why is the vel important?`, a: `It is the spear given to Murugan by Parvati, said to destroy the demon Surapadma and standing for wisdom that overcomes ignorance.` },
    ],
  },
  'ratha-saptami': {
    about: [
      `Ratha Saptami is the day devoted to the Sun god, and the words mean the seventh day of the chariot. Surya rides a chariot drawn by seven horses, often said to stand for the seven colours of light or the seven days of the week, and on this day, in the bright half of Magha, he is said to turn the chariot toward the north and begin the warmer half of the year. The day is also treated as the birthday of Surya, Surya Jayanti, and the chariot is a recurrent image in temple art.`,
      `The distinctive practice, which the data describes, is the bath with seven arka leaves, from the madar plant, placed on the head and shoulders, often along with rice grains and turmeric, with a verse addressed to the Sun. Another custom, particularly in Andhra, is to draw a chariot in coloured powder in the courtyard and cook sweet pongal in a pot in the direction of the sun, offering it with milk. Tirumala holds a special procession, in which the deity Venkateswara is taken around on seven different vahanas in a single day, which is called the Surya Jayanti or Ratha Saptami festival there.`,
      `Famous Sun temples such as Konark in Odisha, Modhera in Gujarat and Arasavalli in Andhra are linked to the occasion, and Arasavalli in particular draws devotees. The Aditya Hridayam, the hymn to the Sun recited in the Ramayana before Rama's battle with Ravana, is a natural choice for the day. Surya is traditionally associated with health and eyesight, but devotees should still see a doctor for any medical condition, treating the prayers as a spiritual practice and not as treatment.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What are arka leaves?`, a: `They are the leaves of the madar or calotropis plant, used in the traditional Ratha Saptami bath in a set of seven.` },
      { q: `Which hymn is recited to Surya?`, a: `The Aditya Hridayam from the Ramayana is the most commonly recited, along with the Surya Namaskara mantras.` },
    ],
  },
  'gangaur': {
    about: [
      `Gangaur is Rajasthan's festival of Gauri, a form of Parvati, and of her husband Isar, a regional name for Shiva. The name joins gana, a name of Shiva, and gaur, a name of Gauri. The festival begins the day after Holi and lasts for about two weeks, ending on the third day of the bright half of Chaitra. Unmarried girls pray for a good husband, and married women pray for the wellbeing of the household.`,
      `Over the days, women sow barley or wheat in small pots and tend the sprouts, which stand for fertility and fresh growth. Clay images of Isar and Gauri are made or bought, dressed in fine cloth and jewellery, and worshipped each morning with songs sung by groups of women who gather with water pots, grass and flowers. On the last day the images are carried in a procession, and then, in the evening, immersed in a pond or well. Songs of the type known as ghoomar and gangaur geet are part of the celebrations.`,
      `The processions in Jaipur, Udaipur and Bikaner are famous, with the one in Jaipur beginning near the City Palace. Udaipur's procession is held at Gangaur Ghat on Lake Pichola. In Mewar and Marwar the festival is intertwined with the love story of Shiva and Parvati, in which she undergoes penance to win him. Related festivals include Gauri Tritiya in Karnataka and Andhra, and in Madhya Pradesh the Nimad area has its own Gangaur. Women generally take pride in their finest clothes and mehndi, and the festival is largely a women's celebration, with men watching from the edges.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Who is Isar in the Gangaur festival?`, a: `Isar is the Rajasthani name for Shiva, the husband of Gauri, whose images are worshipped together.` },
      { q: `Why do women sow barley during Gangaur?`, a: `The sprouting grain stands for fertility and prosperity and is used in the daily worship and in the final procession.` },
    ],
  },
  'gita-jayanti': {
    about: [
      `Gita Jayanti marks the day on which, according to tradition, Krishna taught the Bhagavad Gita to Arjuna in the opening of the Kurukshetra war. The text is a chapter of the Mahabharata's Bhishma Parva, and its seven hundred verses are arranged in eighteen chapters, beginning with Arjuna's despair on the battlefield and ending with Krishna's call for surrender. Unlike most scriptures, the Gita is celebrated on a birthday, which signals its status as a text for daily living.`,
      `The data places the day on the Shukla Ekadashi of Margashirsha, so it is observed together with Mokshada Ekadashi. Different schools of the Gita's interpretation are well known. Shankara's commentary stresses knowledge, Ramanuja's devotion, and Madhva's the difference between the soul and God, while modern readers such as Tilak and Gandhi stressed action and selfless service. The observance is thus open to many readings and readers.`,
      `In Kurukshetra, the Haryana government hosts an international Gita Mahotsav each year with exhibitions, and the Brahma Sarovar is a focus of the gathering. Schools, temples and cultural groups hold group recitations, essay contests and discourses. Some devotees read all eighteen chapters in a single sitting. Many editions of the Gita exist in nearly every language, and a person who has never opened it can start with Chapter Two, which lays out the central ideas of the self and of duty without anxiety about results. The data says it is translated into many languages, which makes it accessible to nearly any reader.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `How many verses does the Gita have?`, a: `The commonly accepted text has seven hundred verses in eighteen chapters, though some manuscripts differ slightly in numbering.` },
      { q: `Where in the Mahabharata is the Gita?`, a: `It lies in the Bhishma Parva, the sixth book, shortly before the fighting starts at Kurukshetra.` },
      { q: `Where should a beginner start reading?`, a: `Many teachers suggest Chapter Two, which introduces the self, duty and action without attachment, before reading the rest in order.` },
    ],
  },
  'karthigai-deepam': {
    about: [
      `Karthigai Deepam is the festival of lamps of Tamil Nadu, and it is linked with Shiva as a column of fire. The story, found in the Puranas, tells that Brahma and Vishnu argued over who was greater, and Shiva appeared as an endless pillar of light. Vishnu in the form of a boar dug down and Brahma as a swan flew upward, and neither found an end. At Tiruvannamalai the hill Arunachala is regarded as that pillar of fire, and the Annamalaiyar temple stands at its foot.`,
      `The climactic event, as the data describes, is the lighting of the Mahadeepam on the summit of the hill at dusk, in a huge cauldron with ghee and a wick, visible for miles around. Before that, the temple holds a ceremony in which the five elements are represented, and the deity Ardhanarishvara appears. Pilgrims also walk around the hill, a fourteen-kilometre path known as giri pradakshina. The date is when the full moon coincides with the Krittika nakshatra in the Tamil month of Karthigai.`,
      `At home, rows of earthen lamps, the agal vilakku, are lit at the threshold, along the windowsills and along the roof edge. Sweets like appam, adai and pori urundai made from puffed rice and jaggery are prepared. Early Tamil literature is often cited as evidence that a festival of lamps in this season is very old, though the exact references are best checked in a scholarly source. Tamil communities in Sri Lanka also light lamps on the same night.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is the Mahadeepam?`, a: `It is the great beacon lit on top of Arunachala hill in Tiruvannamalai at dusk on the festival day, representing Shiva as a pillar of fire.` },
      { q: `Why are so many lamps lit in homes?`, a: `The light stands for Shiva's jyoti form and the removal of darkness, and rows of lamps are lit on thresholds and windowsills.` },
    ],
  },
  'skanda-shashti': {
    about: [
      `Skanda Shashti is the sixth-day observance for Murugan, also known as Skanda, Kartikeya or Subramanya. The shashti, the sixth lunar day of the bright half, recurs each month, but the one in the Tamil month of Aippasi is regarded as the main festival. It leads to the Soorasamharam, the destruction of the demon Surapadma, a story told in the Skanda Purana and in Kachiyappa Sivacharyar's Tamil Kanda Puranam.`,
      `In the story, the asura Surapadma and his brothers oppress the gods after obtaining a boon from Shiva, and the gods turn to Shiva for help. Shiva's spark becomes six children who are raised by the six Krittika women and later united into Murugan, who is given a vel by Parvati. In battle, Murugan splits Surapadma, who is then transformed into a peacock, the mount of the god, and a rooster, which appears on his banner. The festival is thus not merely a tale of destruction but of conversion.`,
      `At Tiruchendur, on the shore of the Bay of Bengal, a six-day festival ends with a public reenactment of the battle on the beach, drawing very large crowds. Many devotees keep a vow for six days, eating a single meal or subsisting on fruit and milk, and recite the Kanda Shashti Kavacham, a hymn attributed to Devaraya Swamigal. The Tamil diaspora in Malaysia, Singapore and Sri Lanka also observes the festival at Murugan temples. Fasting rules vary and should be adapted to health. The festival is read by teachers as a symbol for overcoming internal faults such as ego, rather than as a means to material goals.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is Soorasamharam?`, a: `It is the reenactment of Murugan's victory over the asura Surapadma, performed at Tiruchendur on the last day of Skanda Shashti.` },
      { q: `What is the Kanda Shashti Kavacham?`, a: `It is a Tamil devotional hymn to Murugan that many devotees recite during the six-day vow.` },
    ],
  },
  'chaitra-navaratri': {
    about: [
      `Chaitra Navaratri is the spring counterpart of the better-known autumn Navaratri, and it opens the Hindu year in much of the north and the Deccan. It begins on Chaitra Shukla Pratipada and runs for nine tithis. Because the same day is Ugadi and Gudi Padwa in other regions, the festival often begins with a family ritual of installing a pot, the ghatasthapana, filled with water and sown with barley, which is a symbol of the Goddess's presence.`,
      `The nine days are dedicated to the nine forms of Durga, and the principal text read is the Devi Mahatmya. Because the ninth day is Rama Navami, the spring festival is also called Rama Navaratri in some regions, and Ramayana recitals are common. The Goddess shrines in the Himalayan foothills, including Vaishno Devi, Jwalamukhi and Chintpurni named in the data, are especially busy. Kumari puja, the worship of young girls as forms of the Goddess, is performed on the eighth or ninth day by many households.`,
      `A practical difference is that Chaitra Navaratri tends to be a more quiet and domestic observance than the festive autumn one, with fewer public dances and pandals. Fasting practices overlap, with simple vegetarian foods, rock salt and fruit being typical, but many families who fast in autumn observe the spring one differently. Some devotees follow a full nine-day fast while others fast for the first and last days only. Because the season also marks the arrival of summer, doctors suggest hydration and light meals. The text of the festival is the same Devi Mahatmya read throughout the year.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is ghatasthapana?`, a: `It is the installation of a sanctified pot on the first day, with barley sown around it, to invite the presence of the Goddess for nine days.` },
      { q: `Why is it called Rama Navaratri?`, a: `Because the ninth day coincides with Rama Navami, the birthday of Rama, and some traditions focus on Rama during these days.` },
    ],
  },
  'ahoi-ashtami': {
    about: [
      `Ahoi Ashtami is a mothers' fast, kept in north India for the wellbeing of children. It falls on the eighth day of the dark half of Kartika, a few days before Dhanteras, and in the calendar sits about eight days after Karwa Chauth. Traditionally it was kept for sons, though many families today observe it for all their children. The word ahoi is usually linked to the idea of avoiding a mishap, anhoni, and to the goddess who is asked to turn it away.`,
      `The story told in the data concerns a woman who, while digging clay for the walls of her house, accidentally killed a young porcupine with her tool. After that, her sons died one by one, and in distress she confessed to an old woman. She was advised to draw the image of the animal and to pray to the goddess, which she did, and her children were restored. Variations of the tale exist, and families usually follow the version told by their elders.`,
      `A picture of Ahoi Mata with the cubs is drawn on a wall or on paper, often with eight corners, and the mother fasts until the stars appear, though in some families it is the moon. A silver locket called a syau, which is strung with a bead for each child, is worshipped and later worn or kept. Water is offered to the stars as the fast is broken. Because the moon rises late on this night, some families break the fast when the stars appear. Women with health concerns can adapt the fast, as is true of every vrata, and the intention of care for the children is what counts.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is the syau?`, a: `It is a silver pendant or set of beads used in the worship of Ahoi Mata and kept as a family keepsake.` },
      { q: `Is the fast broken at moonrise or when stars appear?`, a: `Most families break it on seeing the stars, whereas some wait for the moon, which rises late on this night.` },
    ],
  },
  'varuthini-ekadashi': {
    about: [
      `Varuthini Ekadashi is the Ekadashi of the dark half of Vaishakha. The name is explained as coming from varutha, a Sanskrit word for armour, and the day is therefore taken as one that gives protection. The Bhavishyottara Purana is the text usually cited, where Krishna recounts its greatness to Yudhishthira, as with many other Ekadashi mahatmyas that follow the same question-and-answer frame.`,
      `The story attached to it is that of King Mandhata, who was practising austerities in a forest when a bear attacked him and began to drag him away. Unable to fight because of his vow of non-violence, the king prayed to Vishnu, who appeared as Varaha, the boar, and killed the animal. The king's leg had been injured, and Vishnu advised him to observe the Varuthini Ekadashi with devotion, upon which he was healed. Some tellings add that the bear too reached a good state, which is the compassionate point of the tale.`,
      `Observance follows the usual Ekadashi pattern. Grains and beans are avoided, the day is spent in remembrance of Vishnu, often by reading the Vishnu Sahasranama or the Bhagavata, and the fast is broken on Dwadashi morning. Giving food or grain on the following morning is described in the text as especially worthwhile. The vrata is prominent among Vaishnava communities across the north, and in Vrindavan and Udupi. A person who is old, pregnant or ill can substitute fruit and milk for a strict fast. The tradition's own suggestion is that the quality of remembrance matters more than the details of austerity. Because Ekadashi starts and ends according to the local sunrise, each city's panchang may give slightly different timings.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What does Varuthini mean?`, a: `It is commonly derived from varutha, meaning armour, and so the Ekadashi is understood as one that offers protection.` },
      { q: `Which Purana describes this Ekadashi?`, a: `The Bhavishyottara Purana is the usual source, with Krishna narrating its significance to Yudhishthira.` },
    ],
  },
  'vaikuntha-ekadashi': {
    about: [
      `Vaikuntha Ekadashi, also known as Mukkoti Ekadashi, is the most prominent Ekadashi in the Vaishnava calendar of South India. It falls in the bright half of the month of Margashirsha or Pausha, depending on whether the Tamil, Telugu or north Indian calendar is followed, a point the data notes. The core belief is that on this day the northern gate of Vishnu's abode, the Vaikuntha Dwaram, is open, and the devotee who enters the temple through it with devotion gains a special merit.`,
      `In Srirangam, the Sri Ranganathaswamy temple holds the Vaikuntha Ekadashi festival as part of a twenty-one day festival called Adhyayana Utsavam, in which the verses of the Azhvars, the Tamil Vaishnava saints, are recited before the deity. On Ekadashi itself the Paramapada Vasal, the gate of heaven, is opened and the processional image passes through it followed by thousands of pilgrims. At Tirumala, a special northern gate is opened for the occasion, and the crowds are among the largest of the year. Local temples across Tamil Nadu and Andhra replicate the ritual with temporary gateways.`,
      `The legend usually told is that Vishnu defeated the demons Madhu and Kaitabha on this day and granted them liberation, and the gate was then opened for all. Another account links it to the celestial beings who were given the chance to meet the Lord. Devotees keep a fast, remain awake overnight with Vishnu Sahasranama and Tiruppavai, and break the fast the next morning on Dwadashi. Because crowds at major temples are very large on this day, many people choose a smaller local temple and plan their visit in advance. The spiritual teaching is that the gate is a symbol and is available to the sincere mind at any time.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What is the Vaikuntha Dwaram?`, a: `It is the special gate or passage, opened on this day in many Vishnu temples, that represents the entry to Vaikuntha.` },
      { q: `Is Vaikuntha Ekadashi the same as Mukkoti Ekadashi?`, a: `Yes, in Andhra and Telangana the name Mukkoti Ekadashi is used for the same observance.` },
    ],
  },
  'nirjala-ekadashi': {
    about: [
      `Among the twenty-four Ekadashis in a typical year, Nirjala is the hardest, since tradition asks the observer to abstain from water as well as food. It falls in the bright half of Jyeshtha, in the heat before the monsoon. The alternate name, Bhimseni or Pandava Ekadashi, comes from the Mahabharata account of Bhima, who found fasting impossible because of his hunger, and was advised by Vyasa to observe this one Ekadashi strictly in the hope of receiving the merit of all of them.`,
      `The fast itself is demanding and the tradition accepts that not everyone can do it. Those with illness, those who are elderly, pregnant women and children are not expected to go without water, and they may take fruit and milk or water in moderation. In practice, many people keep a partial form with only water allowed, or pass the day with a light satvic diet. The tradition itself regards sincerity and remembrance of Vishnu as the point. Medical advice is particularly sensible in summer heat, when dehydration is a risk.`,
      `In north India, especially in Delhi, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh and Gujarat, the day is marked by the charity of drinking water. Stalls give out cold water, sherbet, mango and pots of water, and clay pots, fans, umbrellas and sandals are given as gifts. This is the social side of the festival and matches its season. The Brahma Vaivarta Purana is among the texts cited. After sunrise on Dwadashi, the fast is broken with a simple meal after offering a share to a needy person. Many temples keep the day quiet, with readings of the Bhagavata.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is it called Bhimseni Ekadashi?`, a: `Because according to the Mahabharata tradition, Bhima, unable to fast regularly, was told to observe this one Ekadashi to gain the benefit of the others.` },
      { q: `Who can skip the waterless fast?`, a: `Children, the elderly, the sick and pregnant women are generally not expected to fast without water, and they may take fruit, milk or water.` },
    ],
  },
  'hartalika-teej': {
    about: [
      `Hartalika Teej is a women's vow dedicated to Shiva and Parvati, kept on the third day of the bright half of Bhadrapada. The name is a compound of harat, abduction, and aalika, a female friend, and it refers to the story in which Parvati's friends took her into the forest to prevent her father from marrying her to Vishnu. There she practised austerities, building a sand linga and worshipping it, until Shiva accepted her. The Puranic accounts differ in detail, but this is the core of the tale.`,
      `Of the three Teej festivals, Hariyali Teej in Shravana, Kajari Teej and Hartalika Teej in Bhadrapada, this is the one associated with strict fasting. Women in Nepal, Bihar, Jharkhand, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan and Madhya Pradesh dress in red and green, apply henna, and sing Teej songs. In Nepal, women gather at the Pashupatinath temple in Kathmandu in red saris, and dancing and singing are an important part of the day. The data states it is a nirjala fast, which means without water, although practice varies by region and household.`,
      `Sand or clay images of Shiva, Parvati and Ganesha are prepared and worshipped through the night, with songs and stories. Married women pray for the wellbeing of their husbands, and unmarried girls hope for a good match. In Maharashtra and Karnataka the same date is observed in a different form as Gowri Habba, where women worship Gauri with a swing and sweets, and the day before Ganesh Chaturthi. The night vigil and the strict fast are not required for health reasons in all families, and modified fasts with fruit or water are widely accepted. The prayers matter more than the austerity, as many elders say.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is it called Hartalika?`, a: `The name combines the words for abduction and a female friend, recalling the friends who took Parvati away to the forest in the story.` },
      { q: `Is the fast kept without water?`, a: `The traditional form is waterless, but many women keep a modified fast, and this is accepted, especially for health reasons.` },
    ],
  },
  'anant-chaturdashi': {
    about: [
      `Anant Chaturdashi has two faces. For many people in western India it is the last day of Ganeshotsav, when processions carry images of Ganesha to the water for immersion. For older Vaishnava households it is the day of the Anant Vrata, in which Vishnu is worshipped as Ananta, the endless one, and a protective thread is tied on the arm. The date is the fourteenth day of the bright half of Bhadrapada, as the data notes.`,
      `The Anant thread has fourteen knots, which are explained as the fourteen worlds of the traditional cosmology. It is dyed with turmeric, consecrated in a ceremony, and worn for a period, commonly until the next year, though practices vary. The vrata story as told in the Bhavishya Purana features a Brahmin named Sumanta and his daughter Sushila, whose husband Kaundinya once mocked the thread, and then lost his prosperity. After he sought out the Lord Ananta and repented, his fortune was restored. A version in the Mahabharata tradition has Krishna advising Yudhishthira to take the vow during the Pandavas' hardship.`,
      `In households, the offering of fourteen kinds of sweets or pooris is a common practice, and a kalash with a representation of Shesha is arranged. In Mumbai and Pune, the immersion processions of Ganesha continue until late at night. In Odisha and parts of Chhattisgarh and Madhya Pradesh, the Anant vrata continues strongly. The data says the fast is phalahar, which means fruit only, in some households. Those attending immersion processions should follow local safety guidance and be mindful of water pollution, using eco-friendly images where possible. The wearing of the Anant thread is optional in practice, and families follow their own tradition.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why does the Anant thread have fourteen knots?`, a: `They are commonly interpreted as the fourteen worlds, or lokas, that Vishnu as Ananta is said to support.` },
      { q: `Is Anant Chaturdashi connected to Ganesh Chaturthi?`, a: `Yes, in Maharashtra and other regions it is the last day of the Ganesh festival and the day of final immersion, while it is also an independent Vishnu vrata.` },
    ],
  },
  'jaya-ekadashi': {
    about: [
      `Jaya Ekadashi, the Ekadashi of victory, belongs to the bright half of Magha. The Padma Purana relates its greatness through a story of two heavenly beings, the gandharva Malyavan and the apsara Pushpavati, who were so absorbed in each other during a performance before Indra that they lost their concentration. Indra cursed them to be reborn as pisachas, spirits of a low order, and they wandered in misery in the Himalayas. By chance, on the Ekadashi of Magha they had no food and stayed up through the cold night, and thus unwittingly kept the vrata.`,
      `Because of that accidental observance they were freed from the curse and returned to heaven. In the data the pair is named differently, so the names vary across tellings and readers may find several spellings of the same story. What stays constant is the message that a good practice, even undertaken without full knowledge, carries a result and that the grace of Vishnu is available to a person in distress. Teachers also read the story as a reminder that wrongdoing is not final.`,
      `As with other Ekadashis, grains are avoided and the day is spent in worship, with the Vishnu Sahasranama and the Jaya Ekadashi katha. The data mentions a satvic meal on the day before and a parana on the morning after Dwadashi begins. Tulsi leaves are offered to Vishnu, and the night is spent in singing. The fast may be adjusted for health, as is accepted in the tradition. Magha is also the month of dawn river baths in the north, so Jaya Ekadashi often falls in the middle of a month of discipline. Some regions treat this Ekadashi alongside Bhishma Ekadashi, an observance on a nearby date that is associated with Bhishma's Vishnu Sahasranama.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What does Jaya mean in Jaya Ekadashi?`, a: `Jaya means victory, and the name points to the victory of the soul over a lower birth or a curse, as in the story from the Padma Purana.` },
      { q: `Which text tells the story of this Ekadashi?`, a: `It appears in the Padma Purana, where Krishna narrates it to Yudhishthira.` },
    ],
  },
  'mauni-amavasya': {
    about: [
      `Mauni Amavasya joins two ideas, mauna, silence, and amavasya, the new moon. It falls on the new moon of Magha, and the rule of the day is a holy bath in a river at dawn, taken in silence. The sage Manu, from whom humanity takes its name in tradition, is sometimes linked to it, and the Puranic sources describe a Magha bathing season in which this day is the highest point. The word muni, a sage, is also related, so the day is for observing the vow of a muni.`,
      `The place most associated with it is Prayagraj, where the Ganga and Yamuna meet, and the Magha Mela is held every year on the riverbank. At the Kumbh and Maha Kumbh, this new moon has long been one of the main bathing dates, and the akharas go in procession to the water in a set order. Pilgrims stay in tents for the month in a practice called kalpavas, living simply and bathing daily. Large crowds on the main bathing day are managed by the administration, and anyone attending should follow guidance on timings and exits.`,
      `Outside the sangam, people bathe in whichever river or tank is nearby, or at home with a prayer to the Ganga. Silence is kept at least until the bath is over, and some people extend it through the day. It is also a day for offering water and sesame to ancestors, since amavasya is the tithi associated with the pitris. Giving food, blankets or sesame to the poor is common in the cold season. Mauna is not meant as a punishment, and teachers recommend using the quiet for japa or simple attention. A silent vow may be kept for any length that suits a person.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What does Mauni Amavasya mean?`, a: `Mauni derives from mauna, silence, and the day is the Magha new moon on which a silent holy bath is customary.` },
      { q: `Where is the main bathing observed?`, a: `Prayagraj at the confluence of the Ganga and Yamuna is the best known place, particularly during the Magha Mela.` },
    ],
  },
  'devotthan-ekadashi': {
    about: [
      `Devotthan Ekadashi, also called Prabodhini Ekadashi and Dev Uthani Gyaras, is the day when Vishnu is said to wake after a four-month sleep. The sleep begins on Devshayani Ekadashi in Ashadha and the intervening months are the Chaturmas, the monsoon retreat season, during which marriages and other major ceremonies are traditionally avoided. The waking is on the Ekadashi of the bright half of Kartika, which is why the day is regarded as the reopening of the auspicious calendar.`,
      `The ritual of awakening is a charming one. In many homes, women draw footprints in rice flour from the door to the shrine to welcome the god, and at dawn or in the evening the household sings songs asking the god to wake, such as Uthho Dev Baitho Dev in the north. Sugarcane stalks are tied to make a small canopy over the image, and fruits, water chestnuts and sweet potatoes of the season are offered. Gonds and several communities in central India have their own songs and observances. In Maharashtra, the town of Pandharpur holds one of its four major pilgrimages on this day for the deity Vitthal.`,
      `The Ekadashi is followed by Tulsi Vivah, and from this point engagements and weddings are scheduled in large numbers in many communities. The day is observed with a fast, from grains in most households, while some take only fruit. Monks and householders who took Chaturmas vows, such as giving up a food or sleeping on the floor, may finish them on this day. As noted in the data, the date is a partial fast, and ordinary guidance applies: observe within your means. The idea of the god being awakened is understood by teachers as a poetic way to invite one's own attention back to life after a quiet season.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is it called Devotthan?`, a: `Devotthan means the rising of the god, and refers to Vishnu waking from his four-month yoga nidra.` },
      { q: `What happens after Devotthan Ekadashi?`, a: `Marriages and other auspicious ceremonies resume in the calendar, usually beginning with Tulsi Vivah.` },
    ],
  },
  'akshay-navami': {
    about: [
      `Akshay Navami, or Amla Navami, falls on the ninth day of the bright half of Kartika and centres on the amla tree, the Indian gooseberry. The word akshay means undiminishing, and the day is said in the traditions to give lasting results to acts of giving and worship. The Puranic sources connect the amla tree with Vishnu, and a well-known story says that Lakshmi, travelling on earth, wished to worship both Shiva and Vishnu together and chose the amla, which combines the qualities of the bel and the tulsi.`,
      `The ritual is distinctive. The family goes to an amla tree, in a garden, a temple compound or a park, cleans the base, waters it, winds a thread around the trunk, and offers worship with turmeric, rice and lamps. A meal is then cooked and eaten sitting under the tree, which is the custom that gives the festival its social flavour. In Bengal the day is also associated with Jagaddhatri Puja, and in Braj the period around it is the start of the parikrama season. The data states the date as ending the Treta Yuga cycle in its own phrasing, whereas the Puranas mostly speak of the start of Treta Yuga on Akshaya Tritiya, so readers will find several accounts.`,
      `Amla is rich in vitamin C and has long been valued in Ayurveda, which is likely part of why it is honoured at the start of winter. The festival provides a reminder of the value of trees. Planting an amla sapling is a fitting offering, and giving food or warm clothes to the needy is customary. Where no tree is available, a potted plant or a picture can stand in, and families can eat amla preparations such as murabba at home. Those who cannot visit Braj can undertake any short walk of prayer.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `Why is the amla tree worshipped?`, a: `The Puranas connect it with Vishnu and Lakshmi, and it is valued in Ayurveda, so it is honoured on this day with a meal eaten under its shade.` },
      { q: `Is Akshay Navami the same as Akshaya Tritiya?`, a: `No. Akshaya Tritiya is in Vaishakha, whereas Akshay Navami is in Kartika, though both carry the idea of undiminishing merit.` },
    ],
  },
  'kajari-teej': {
    about: [
      `Kajari Teej, also known as Kajli Teej or Badi Teej, is the Teej of the dark clouds. It is observed on the third day of the dark half of Bhadrapada, in the thick of the monsoon, a few days before Hartalika Teej in the bright half. The name kajari is connected with the black rain clouds and with the song form kajari, sung by women in the Hindi-speaking belt and in Bhojpuri country in the rainy months. Parvati's devotion to Shiva is the religious base.`,
      `The tales told on the day concern a woman who keeps the vow and thereby obtains her husband's welfare, and the Teej stories of Parvati winning Shiva after long penance. As with the other Teej festivals, married women pray for their husbands and unmarried girls for a good match. Swings are hung from trees, and women gather in groups to sing kajari songs, whose themes are longing, separation, the rain and the stories of Radha and Krishna. The data mentions neem and moon-sighting, which are part of the evening worship in many homes.`,
      `Bundi in Rajasthan has a well-known Kajari Teej fair, and in Uttar Pradesh the Mirzapur region is celebrated for its kajari singing, which has also entered the folk-classical repertoire. In Banaras, singers continue the tradition in concerts. Women in Bihar and Madhya Pradesh also keep the vow. In most places barley sattu is the special food, made into sweets and used to break the fast after the moon is seen. The fast may be adapted for health, and the swings, songs and shared meals are the real heart of the day for many families. Mehndi on the hands and green clothes belong to the whole Teej season.`,
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: `What are kajari songs?`, a: `They are folk songs sung in the rainy season, particularly in Uttar Pradesh and Bihar, about longing, rain and the love stories of Radha and Krishna.` },
      { q: `Which three festivals are called Teej?`, a: `Hariyali Teej in Shravana, Kajari Teej in the dark half of Bhadrapada and Hartalika Teej in its bright half.` },
    ],
  },
};
